<CaseLayout title="Basketball decision platform" eyebrow="01 / BASKETBALL · SCOUTING & ROSTER STRATEGY" heading="THE RIGHT PLAYER. THE RIGHT FIT." description="A basketball scouting platform that connects player evaluation, shot analysis, and team constraints in one workflow." role="Scouting interface, configurable evaluation tools, roster scenarios, and analytical workflows within Toledo Athletics’ data internship program." status="Working dashboard / ongoing development" stack="JavaScript · SVG · Cloudflare Workers · D1">
  <section id="question"><h2>A better shortlist starts<br>with a better question.</h2><p>A player’s value depends on the job the team needs done. Coaches need to compare shooting, playmaking, defense, and roster constraints together. A single scoring average cannot tell that whole story.</p><p>I worked on tools that make those tradeoffs visible, then give the person evaluating a player a practical next step.</p></section>
  <section id="approach"><h2>Compare. Question. Construct.</h2><div class="two-col"><div><h3>Make evaluation inspectable</h3><p>Configurable performance scores and role-specific fit presets put different dimensions of a player’s game alongside one another. Dot, hexbin, and zone charts add spatial context.</p></div><div><h3>Carry the decision forward</h3><p>The roster builder enforces roster size, duplicate-player restrictions, league separation, per-player caps, and total budget. A shortlist becomes a team-building exercise.</p></div></div><h3 style="margin-top:32px">Try the idea.</h3><p>This small exercise illustrates how a change in priorities changes a ranking. Its fictional 0–100 inputs and weighted formula are fully visible. It is separate from the dashboard’s production scoring models.</p><ScoutingLab client:visible compact /></section>
  <section id="evidence"><div id="validation"></div><h2>Show the result.<br>Keep the context.</h2><p>The saved MAC Tournament retrospective compares three Toledo games. It is a useful small case for inspecting predictions and uncertainty—not a broad claim of predictive accuracy.</p><div class="metric-grid"><div><strong>1.07</strong><span>Toledo team-score MAE / points</span></div><div><strong>3.63</strong><span>Game-margin MAE / points</span></div><div><strong>3</strong><span>Games in the retrospective</span></div></div><div class="data-table"><table><caption>Toledo score comparison · saved 2026 MAC Tournament retrospective</caption><thead><tr><th>Opponent</th><th>Absolute Toledo score error</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bowling Green</td><td>0.3 points</td><td>Quarterfinal forecast near 50/50</td></tr><tr><td>UMass</td><td>0.9 points</td><td>Margin error was larger: 8.6 points</td></tr><tr><td>Akron</td><td>2.0 points</td><td>Final margin within about 1.1 points</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="evidence-note"><strong>A note on the original report</strong><p>The source PDF uses inconsistent winner-pick language. Its Bowling Green forecast slightly favors BG, while Toledo won. “Three out of three winners correctly predicted” is not supported by those favorite picks. The metrics above retain the full three-game scope.</p></div><p>Toledo Athletics’ April 20, 2026 feature names me among the students working on the basketball project and describes the program’s practical work with athletics staff.</p><a class="text-link" href={articleUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the Athletics feature ↗</a></section>
  <section id="limitations"><h2>A decision aid, with limits.</h2><p>The tournament sample is too small to establish general calibration. Player valuations are model outputs, not verified market prices. Upstream data coverage and freshness also affect the quality of an evaluation.</p><p>The next step is broader, prospectively recorded validation and continued work on making the assumptions behind each recommendation easy to inspect.</p></section>
